{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויגז", :translit "va-yiggoz", :gloss "and he sheared / and it sheared"} {:he "ויגז שלוים", :translit "va-yiggoz slavim", :gloss "and it sheared quail"} {:he "ויגז ראשו", :translit "va-yiggoz rosho", :gloss "and he sheared his head"} {:he "שלוים", :translit "shelavim", :gloss "quail (ketiv)"} {:he "סלוים", :translit "slavim", :gloss "quail (qere)"} {:he "שלוים לצדיקים", :translit "shelavim la-tzaddikim", :gloss "peaceful-ones for the righteous"} {:he "סלוים לרשעים", :translit "slavim la-resha'im", :gloss "thorn-ones for the wicked"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ויגז שלוים" "ויגז ראשו"]}], :frame {:text "The midrash compares the quail catastrophe in Numbers 11 to Job's mourning: just as Job mourned his sons who died when a house fell upon them (Job 1:19), so Israel mourned those who died when the quail fell upon them. The midrash further notes that Moses killed the people through eating, while the quail themselves killed through falling. The ketiv/qere distinction is read as a double vision: the written form שלוים (from שלום, peace) marks the quail as 'peaceful ones' for the righteous, while the qere form סלוים (from סלה, thorn/basket) marks them as 'thorn-like' for the wicked.", :sources ["Job 1:19" "Numbers 11:31-34"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ויגז שלוים" "ויגז ראשו"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ויגז שלוים", :refs ["Numbers 11:31"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "ויגז ראשו", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive}]}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:28:32.251392128Z", :model "glm-5.2"}